<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM "../test.dtd">

<suite id = expressions-tests title = "Expression Tests">

<category id = literals title = "Literals">

    <prose>Expressions are the core computational units of Scaly. Every expression evaluates to a value.

    <tests id = boolean-literals title = "Boolean Literals">
        <prose>The boolean literals are true and false.
        <test id = bool-true><input>true<expect>true
        <test id = bool-false><input>false<expect>false

    <tests id = integer-literals title = "Integer Literals">
        <prose>Integer literals are sequences of decimal digits, optionally preceded by a minus sign for negative numbers.
        <test id = int-positive><input>42<expect>42
        <test id = int-zero><input>0<expect>0
        <test id = int-negative><input>-7<expect>-7

    <tests id = hex-literals title = "Hexadecimal Literals">
        <prose>Hexadecimal literals start with 0x or 0X followed by hexadecimal digits (0-9, a-f, A-F).
        <test id = hex-ff><input>0xFF<expect>255
        <test id = hex-1a><input>0x1A<expect>26
        <test id = hex-zero><input>0x0<expect>0

    <tests id = float-literals title = "Floating-Point Literals">
        <prose>Floating-point literals contain a decimal point separating the integer and fractional parts.
        <test id = float-pi><input>3.14<expect>3.14
        <test id = float-two><input>2.0<expect>2.0
        <test id = float-half><input>0.5<expect>0.5

    <tests id = string-literals title = "String Literals">
        <prose>String literals are enclosed in double quotes. They may span multiple lines, with line breaks preserved in the resulting string.
        <test id = string-hello><input>"hello"<expect>hello
        <test id = string-empty><input>""<expect>

    <tests id = character-literals title = "Character Literals">
        <prose>A character is written as a string literal of one character wherever a char is expected: a char parameter, a typed binding, or the cast `as char`.
        <test id = char-a><input>"a" as char<expect>a
        <test id = char-z><input>"Z" as char<expect>Z

<category id = operations title = "Operations">

    <prose>An operation is a sequence of things: values, names, operator names, tuples in parentheses, vector literals in brackets and blocks in braces, each perhaps followed by a member access with a dot. The compiler reads the sequence from left to right and holds one current value; the TYPE of that value says what the next thing means. A tuple after a function is the call, a tuple after a type the construction, a vector literal after a value the indexing, an operator name after a value one of the operators its type declares. Parentheses, brackets, braces and the dot are what structure a sequence, and nothing else does. An operation ends with a colon, a line break, or anything that is not part of it.

    <tests id = prefix-functions title = "Prefix Operators">
        <prose>Where a value is expected an operator name is a prefix: it takes the next value as its operand. A value is expected at the start of an operation, after an operator, and after a function, so `a - -2` subtracts minus two and `twice -5` is `twice(-5)`. An `=` at the start of an operation does nothing, so `let a = 42` reads as `let a 42`.
        <test id = negate-positive><input>-7<expect>-7
        <test id = negate-negative><input>-(-3)<expect>3
        <test id = prefix-add><input>+ 3<expect>3
        <test id = prefix-after-operator><input>10 - -2<expect>12
        <test id = prefix-after-function program = program><input>function twice(n: int) returns int
    n * 2
twice -5<expect>-10
        <test id = leading-equals><input>= 6 * 7<expect>42

    <tests id = operator-chains title = "Operator Chains">
        <prose>Several operators in a row combine by their binding strength, which the standard library states as a protocol of pending types: the compiler itself has no precedence table. Strongest first, the levels are `*` `/` `%` `div` `mod`; `+` `-`; `<<` `>>`; `<` `>` `<=` `>=`; `=` `&#60;&#62;`; `&`; `xor`; `|`; `and` `&&`; `or` `||`. An operator a type declares that no level names, such as `==` or a text name, binds most weakly of all. Operators of one level combine from left to right.
        <test id = simple-add><input>3 + 4<expect>7
        <test id = simple-subtract><input>10 - 3<expect>7
        <test id = simple-multiply><input>6 * 7<expect>42
        <test id = simple-divide><input>84 / 2<expect>42
        <test id = simple-modulo><input>17 % 5<expect>2
        <test id = chain-with-precedence><input>3 + 4 * 2<expect>11
        <test id = chain-left-to-right><input>20 - 5 - 3<expect>12
        <test id = shift-below-sum><input>1 << 2 + 1<expect>8
        <test id = comparison-below-arithmetic><input>1 + 2 * 3 = 7<expect>true
        <test id = and-below-comparison><input>1 < 2 and 3 < 4<expect>true

    <tests id = application title = "Application Before Operators">
        <prose>Before an operator takes the thing that follows it, that thing takes what is applied to it: a function its tuple, a value its vector literal, anything its members. So `a + f(1)` adds the call's result, and `a + v[i]` the element. A function called without parentheses takes exactly one thing; an argument that is itself a call, an indexing or a construction takes its parentheses, `print(f(x))`.
        <test id = call-before-operator program = program><input>function twice(n: int) returns int
    n * 2
3 + twice(4) * 2<expect>19
        <test id = parenless-call program = program><input>function twice(n: int) returns int
    n * 2
twice 21<expect>42

    <tests id = parentheses-grouping title = "Parentheses and Grouping">
        <prose>Parentheses around a single value group it: `(a)` is `a`. The group is read as an operation of its own, so a prefix may begin it inside a longer chain, and it combines first.
        <test id = parens-negate><input>5 * (- 2)<expect>-10
        <test id = parens-grouping><input>2 * (3 + 4)<expect>14
        <test id = nested-parens><input>(2 + 3) * (4 + 1)<expect>25

    <tests id = casts-whole-operation title = "Casts Take the Whole Operation">
        <prose>A cast `as T` and a test `is T` apply to the whole operation before them: `a + b as T` is `(a + b) as T`. A cast of the right side alone takes parentheses.
        <test id = cast-after-sum program = program><input>let a: u8 200
let b: u8 100
a + b as int<expect>44
        <test id = cast-of-right-side program = program><input>let a: u8 200
let b: u8 100
a + (b as int)<expect>300

    <tests id = identifier-shapes title = "Identifier Shapes">
        <prose>Identifiers can have name shape (alphanumeric) or operator shape (symbols). The shape is purely lexical and does not determine behavior: what a name means depends on its declaration and on what the operation holds when it is reached.
        <test id = name-as-operator><input>10 div 3<expect>3
        <test id = name-as-function><input>abs (- 5)<expect>5

    <tests id = let-bindings title = "Let Bindings">
        <prose>Let bindings introduce local variables. The syntax is let followed by a name and a value expression (no equals sign). Multiple statements are separated by colons or newlines.
        <test id = let-simple program = program><input>let x 5
x<expect>5
        <test id = let-use-binding program = program><input>let x 5
x + 3<expect>8
        <test id = let-nested program = program><input>let x 5
let y 3
x + y<expect>8
        <test id = let-expr-value program = program><input>let x 2 + 3
x * 2<expect>10

</category>

<category id = tuples title = "Tuples and Function Values">

    <prose>A tuple is a value: an anonymous record written in parentheses. Its components may carry labels, and its type is written the same way, `(int, String)`. The empty tuple `()` is the type of no value, which a routine without a result answers. Functions are values too: a name bound to a function, or a method bound to its receiver, is called later like the function itself.

    <tests id = tuple-values title = "Tuples">
        <prose>A labelled component is read by its label. Unlabelled components are reached by decomposition, `let (a, b) t`; a tuple is not an array and has no indexing. A routine returns several values as a tuple.
        <test id = tuple-labels program = program><input>let t (x: 3, y: 4)
t.x * t.y<expect>12
        <test id = tuple-decompose program = program><input>let (a, b) (6, 7)
a * b<expect>42
        <test id = tuple-result program = program><input>function pair() returns (int, int)
    (3, 4)
let (p, q) pair()
p + q<expect>7

    <tests id = tuple-application title = "Applying a Bound Tuple">
        <prose>A tuple bound to a name stands for its components where it is applied: `f args` calls f with them, and `Point(args)` builds the record from them. Its components are evaluated once, when it is bound.
        <test id = tuple-applied program = program><input>function add(a: int, b: int) returns int
    a + b
let args (40, 2)
add args<expect>42
        <test id = tuple-construction program = program><input>define Point(x: int, y: int)
let args (1, 2)
let p Point(args)
p.y<expect>2

    <tests id = function-values title = "Functions as Values">
        <prose>`let g twice` binds the function at compile time and costs nothing; `g(21)` is a direct call. `x.m` without a tuple is the method bound to its receiver. Where a delegate type is declared, a named function becomes a closure value, chosen by the delegate's signature.
        <test id = function-bound program = program><input>function twice(n: int) returns int
    n * 2
let g twice
g(21)<expect>42
        <test id = method-bound program = program><input>define Counter(n: int)
{
    function plus(this, k: int) returns int
        n + k
}
let c Counter(40)
let m c.plus
m(2)<expect>42
        <test id = function-as-delegate program = program><input>define IntFn delegate(int) returns int
function apply(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int
    f(x)
function twice(n: int) returns int
    n * 2
apply(twice, 21)<expect>42

</category>

<category id = comparison-operators title = "Comparison Operators">

    <prose>Comparison operators compare two values and return a boolean result.

    <tests id = equality title = "Equality">
        <prose>The equality operator (=) tests if two values are equal. The inequality operator (not equal to) tests if two values are different.
        <test id = equal-true><input>5 = 5<expect>true
        <test id = equal-false><input>5 = 3<expect>false
        <test id = not-equal-true><input>5 &#60;&#62; 3<expect>true
        <test id = not-equal-false><input>5 &#60;&#62; 5<expect>false

    <tests id = relational title = "Relational Operators">
        <prose>Relational operators compare the ordering of values.
        <test id = less-than-true><input>3 < 5<expect>true
        <test id = less-than-false><input>5 < 3<expect>false
        <test id = greater-than-true><input>5 > 3<expect>true
        <test id = greater-than-false><input>3 > 5<expect>false
        <test id = less-or-equal-true><input>5 <= 5<expect>true
        <test id = less-or-equal-false><input>5 <= 3<expect>false
        <test id = greater-or-equal-true><input>5 >= 5<expect>true
        <test id = greater-or-equal-false><input>3 >= 5<expect>false

</category>

<category id = boolean-operators title = "Boolean Operators">

    <prose>Boolean operators combine boolean values.

    <tests id = boolean-negation title = "Boolean Negation">
        <prose>The word operator `not` negates a boolean value. The tilde is the bitwise complement, which on a bool is the same. The exclamation mark is not negation in Scaly: it is the sigil of the thrown lifetime.
        <test id = not-true><input>not true<expect>false
        <test id = not-false><input>not false<expect>true
        <test id = negate-true><input>~true<expect>false
        <test id = negate-false><input>~false<expect>true

    <tests id = boolean-and-or title = "Boolean And/Or">
        <prose>The operators `and` and `or` (also spelled `&&` and `||`) are declared by the standard library with a lazy right operand: the right side is evaluated only where the left one does not decide. The bitwise operators `&`, `|` and `xor` combine two bools as well, and evaluate both sides.
        <test id = and-word><input>true and false<expect>false
        <test id = or-word><input>false or true<expect>true
        <test id = and-short-circuit program = program><input>procedure noisy() returns bool
{
    print "evaluated"
    true
}
false and noisy()<expect>false
        <test id = or-short-circuit program = program><input>procedure noisy() returns bool
{
    print "evaluated"
    false
}
true or noisy()<expect>true
        <test id = and-true-true><input>true & true<expect>true
        <test id = and-true-false><input>true & false<expect>false
        <test id = or-false-false><input>false | false<expect>false
        <test id = or-true-false><input>true | false<expect>true
        <test id = xor-bools><input>true xor true<expect>false

</category>

<category id = bitwise-operators title = "Bitwise Operators">

    <prose>Bitwise operators manipulate integer values at the bit level.

    <tests id = bit-shifts title = "Bit Shifts">
        <prose>The left shift operator shifts bits to the left, effectively multiplying by powers of two. The right shift operator shifts bits to the right, effectively dividing by powers of two.
        <test id = left-shift-one><input>1 << 4<expect>16
        <test id = left-shift-five><input>5 << 3<expect>40
        <test id = right-shift-sixteen><input>16 >> 2<expect>4
        <test id = right-shift-hundred><input>100 >> 3<expect>12

</category>

<category id = lambdas title = "Lambdas">

    <prose>A lambda is an anonymous function written with a backslash:
    `\params: body`. Parameters are space-separated. A lambda bound to a name
    and applied to arguments is monomorphized at the call site for those
    argument types, so the same lambda works at any argument type.

    <tests id = lambda-application title = "Lambda Application">
        <prose>A lambda bound to a name can be called like a function.
        <test id = lambda-inc program = program><input>let inc \x: x + 1
inc(5)<expect>6
        <test id = lambda-add program = program><input>let add \x y: x + y
add(3, 4)<expect>7
        <test id = lambda-reused program = program><input>let dbl \x: x + x
let a dbl(10)
let b dbl(21)
a + b<expect>62

    <tests id = lambda-captures title = "Captures">
        <prose>A lambda captures enclosing variables by value. The captured
        value is fixed when the lambda is applied.
        <test id = lambda-capture-one program = program><input>let n 10
let f \x: x + n
f(5)<expect>15
        <test id = lambda-capture-many program = program><input>let a 3
let b 4
let g \x: x + a + b
g(10)<expect>17

    <tests id = lambda-block-captures title = "Captures in Braced Bodies">
        <prose>Capture discovery reaches inside a braced body, and a body-local
        binding that shadows an enclosing name is the local, not a capture.
        <test id = lambda-capture-block program = program><input>let n 10
let cap \x: { x + n }
cap(5)<expect>15
        <test id = lambda-capture-shadow program = program><input>let n 10
let shade \x: { let n 99
x + n }
shade(5)<expect>104

    <tests id = lambda-embedded title = "Embedded Calls">
        <prose>A lambda call may appear anywhere inside a larger expression, not
        only as the whole right-hand side of a binding. Like every application,
        each call takes its arguments before the operators around it combine.
        <test id = lambda-embed-sum program = program><input>let inc \x: x + 1
let add \x y: x + y
inc(5) + add(3, 4)<expect>13
        <test id = lambda-embed-mix program = program><input>let inc \x: x + 1
let n 100
n + inc(9)<expect>110
        <test id = lambda-embed-capture program = program><input>let n 100
let cap \x: x + n
cap(1) + cap(2)<expect>203

    <tests id = lambda-delegates title = "Delegate Values">
        <prose>A `delegate` names a function type. A non-capturing lambda assigned
        to a delegate-typed binding is a function-pointer value that can be passed
        to a function and called indirectly. A lambda literal may also be passed
        straight to a delegate parameter, and so may a named function.
        <test id = delegate-apply program = program><input>define IntFn delegate(int) returns int
function apply(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int
    f(x)
let g: IntFn \x: x + 1
apply(g, 5)<expect>6
        <test id = delegate-twice program = program><input>define IntFn delegate(int) returns int
function twice(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int
    f(f(x))
let dbl: IntFn \x: x + x
twice(dbl, 20)<expect>80

    <tests id = lambda-closures title = "Closures">
        <prose>A capturing lambda assigned to a delegate type is a true closure
        value carrying the captured variables. (Closures returned from a
        function escape onto the caller's region -- see the escaping-closure AOT
        regression test.)
        <test id = closure-capture program = program><input>define IntFn delegate(int) returns int
function apply(f: IntFn, x: int) returns int
    f(x)
let n 10
let add: IntFn \x: x + n
apply(add, 5)<expect>15

</category>

</suite>
